#
# HISTORICAL — MEASURED AT HEAD (2026-08-03), post-`2edb9c06` pre-residual-fix:
#   int y = p[5]              -> error[E_NotIndexable]   (correctly rejected)
#   int y = 0 + p[5]          -> error[E_NotIndexable]   (binop operand: ok)
#   print(p[5])               -> error[E_NotIndexable]   (call arg: ok)
#   print(f"{p[5]}")          -> "OK: no semantic errors"  <-- THE HOLE
#   String s = f"{p[5]}"      -> "OK: no semantic errors"  <-- same hole
#
# And it is not merely a missing diagnostic — the lowering still runs:
#   C    : 11 22 281474067791448
#   LLVM : 11 22 281473633924176
# i.e. p[5] reads OUT OF BOUNDS past a 2-field struct, exactly as before the
# fix. Identical on both backends => >= 2 bugs by Core #8, never "benign".
#
# ROOT: the gate lives at the typecheck `Expr::Index` arm; the f-string
# interpolation path does not route through it. This is a known SIBLING SITE
# in this tree, with precedent: `E_AmpInOperandPosition` had to be applied at
# BOTH `src/semantic/safety/check_expr.rs:349` (main walker) AND `:1592` (the
# f-string interp walker sibling). Track A applied one and missed the other —
# Core #4: fix the class, and the class includes the interp walker.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS WAS EASY TO MISS, recorded because it bit twice: an f-string
# swallows typecheck errors, so AGENTS.md Core #15(d) says never probe
# accept/reject inside one. That rule exists because the probe lies — but the
# same property is ALSO a live compiler defect, and the rule's existence made
